==================================================
38. FORM ERROR UX
==================================================

When validation fails:

- preserve entered fields
- show errors near fields
- scroll/focus first error when practical
- use understandable Arabic messages

Never display Laravel/PHP technical error messages to end users.

==================================================
39. BUTTON LANGUAGE
==================================================

Buttons should describe the action.

Avoid generic:

Submit
OK
Action

Prefer:

حفظ المعاملة
إضافة مصروف
رفع الفاتورة
حفظ والمتابعة
إرسال للمراجعة
إنشاء الإحالة

Use only one strong primary action per context.

==================================================
40. STATUS SYSTEM
==================================================

Status must never depend only on color.

Use:

icon + label + color

Examples:

✓ مكتمل

⏳ بانتظار المراجعة

⚠ يحتاج إجراء

● مسودة

Status naming must remain consistent throughout the platform.

==================================================
41. EMPTY STATES
==================================================

Never display empty blank cards or blank tables.

Example:

لا توجد معاملات مالية حتى الآن.

Then, if authorized:

+ إضافة أول معاملة

==================================================
42. LOADING STATES
==================================================

Every async action needs feedback.

Prevent double submission.

Use:

- spinner
- disabled button
- skeleton only where useful
- progress state

Never leave users wondering whether an action occurred.

==================================================
43. ERROR MESSAGES
==================================================

Users must never see:

SQLSTATE
stack trace
Laravel exception
provider error
filesystem path
API credentials
internal implementation details

Production UI should display a helpful message.

Detailed errors belong in secure logs.

==================================================
44. DASHBOARDS
==================================================

Do not create charts simply because dashboards usually contain charts.

Every dashboard element must answer a business question.

SME dashboard:

- current money overview
- recent activity
- unpaid receivables/payables
- compliance alerts
- readiness progress
- next recommended actions

Beacon dashboard:

- onboarding
- record completeness
- programme progress
- compliance
- readiness
- technical assistance
- gender/geography/sector
- referrals
- outcomes

Use charts only when they improve understanding.

==================================================
45. DATA TABLES
==================================================

Desktop administrative tables must support as appropriate:

- pagination
- search
- filtering
- sorting
- clear row actions

Never load thousands of records at once.

Use server-side pagination.

Avoid exposing irrelevant database fields.

==================================================
46. ACCESSIBILITY
==================================================

Use semantic HTML.

All form fields require labels.

All icon buttons require accessible labels.

Keyboard navigation must work.

Keep focus indicators visible.

Never communicate meaning only through:

- color
- icon
- hover

Maintain adequate contrast.

==================================================
47. AUTHORIZATION
==================================================

Security must always be enforced by Laravel.

Frontend visibility is never authorization.

Use:

- Laravel Policies
- Gates where appropriate
- scoped queries
- permissions

Every sensitive request must verify access.

==================================================
48. USER ROLES
==================================================

At minimum:

SME_USER
BEACON_ADVISOR
BEACON_ADMIN
MFI_USER
TECHNICAL_ADMIN

Use RBAC plus record-level authorization.

Role alone is not sufficient.

==================================================
49. ACCESS RULES
==================================================

SME_USER:

Can access only data belonging to their own authorized enterprise.

BEACON_ADVISOR:

Can access assigned/permitted SMEs.

BEACON_ADMIN:

Can access authorized programme administration and programme records.

MFI_USER:

Can access only referral packages explicitly shared with their own
institution.

TECHNICAL_ADMIN:

Has only documented technical privileges.

Technical admin status must not automatically mean unrestricted
business-data access.

Use principle of least privilege.

==================================================
50. IDOR PREVENTION
==================================================

Prevent Insecure Direct Object Reference vulnerabilities.

Never assume knowing a record ID grants access.

These must always be authorized:

/smes/{id}

/transactions/{id}

/documents/{id}

/assessments/{id}

/referrals/{id}

/exports/{id}

Authorization applies equally to:

- page view
- edit
- delete
- export
- download
- AI retrieval
- AJAX requests

==================================================
51. POLICIES
==================================================

Create policies where appropriate:

SMEPolicy
TransactionPolicy
DocumentPolicy
CompliancePolicy
AssessmentPolicy
ReferralPolicy
ReportPolicy
UserPolicy

Avoid manually duplicating authorization logic in many controllers.

==================================================
52. AUTHENTICATION SECURITY
==================================================

Use Laravel's trusted authentication mechanisms.

Requirements:

- secure password hashing
- login rate limiting
- secure password reset
- session regeneration
- account deactivation
- session revocation where needed

Never implement custom password hashing.

Never implement custom cryptography.

==================================================
53. MFA
==================================================

Multi-factor authentication must be supported for privileged accounts.

At minimum strongly enforce/require MFA for:

BEACON_ADMIN
TECHNICAL_ADMIN

depending on approved operational policy.

Protect MFA secrets and recovery codes.

==================================================
54. SESSION SECURITY
==================================================

Production cookies should be configured securely.

Use as appropriate:

Secure
HttpOnly
SameSite

Session identifiers must regenerate after login.

Revoked or disabled accounts must lose access.

==================================================
55. CSRF
==================================================

All browser state-changing operations require CSRF protection.

Never disable Laravel CSRF globally.

==================================================
56. VALIDATION
==================================================

Every write request must use server-side Laravel validation.

Prefer FormRequest classes.

Example:

CreateTransactionRequest
UpdateTransactionRequest
CreateReferralRequest

Never trust React validation alone.

==================================================
57. MASS ASSIGNMENT
==================================================

Never do:

Model::create($request->all());

Never do:

$model->update($request->all());

Use:

$request->validated()

Sensitive fields must never be unexpectedly assignable.

Examples:

role
permissions
user_id
institution_id
sme_id
approval_status
referral_status

==================================================
58. XSS
==================================================

Treat all user-generated text as untrusted.

Never render raw user/AI HTML without sanitization.

Avoid:

dangerouslySetInnerHTML

unless:

- explicitly necessary
- sanitized
- documented

==================================================
59. SQL INJECTION
==================================================

Prefer:

Eloquent
Query Builder
parameter binding

Never concatenate user input into SQL.

==================================================
60. FILE UPLOAD SECURITY
==================================================

Treat every uploaded file as hostile.

Validate:

- MIME type
- allowed extension
- size
- authorization
- expected purpose

Generate random secure server-side file names.

Never trust the original filename.

Never allow executable uploads.

==================================================
61. PRIVATE DOCUMENT STORAGE
==================================================

Receipts, invoices, registrations and financing documents must be
private.

Never store private SME documents directly under:

public/

Use private Laravel storage or approved private object storage.

Download flow must:

1. authenticate
2. authorize
3. retrieve private file
4. return/stream file

Never expose public predictable URLs.

==================================================
62. DOCUMENT DOWNLOAD SECURITY
==================================================

Every download must enforce the same record-level permissions as the
application screen.

A user unable to view an SME must also be unable to download that
SME's files.

Direct link guessing must not bypass authorization.

==================================================
63. FINANCIAL DATA
==================================================

Never use:

FLOAT
DOUBLE

for money.

Use DECIMAL.

Define precision explicitly.

Store:

- original amount
- currency
- transaction date
- transaction type
- category
- payment status
- created_by

where applicable.

==================================================
64. FINANCIAL AUTHORITY
==================================================

Stored deterministic financial records are authoritative.

AI must never become the authoritative financial calculation engine.

Laravel/PHP/database logic calculates:

- income
- expenses
- P&L
- receivables
- payables
- cash flow
- financial totals

AI may explain those values afterward.

==================================================
65. FINANCIAL CORRECTIONS
==================================================

Do not silently overwrite posted financial records.

Corrections must preserve history.

Store where appropriate:

- original value
- corrected value
- user
- timestamp
- reason

Critical financial operations must use database transactions.

==================================================
66. DOUBLE COUNTING
==================================================

Prevent accounting double counting.

Examples:

Credit sale + later payment collection must not record revenue twice.

Credit purchase + later supplier payment must not record expense twice.

Tests must cover these scenarios.

==================================================
67. DATABASE
==================================================

Use proper relational MySQL design.

Use:

- migrations
- foreign keys
- indexes
- unique constraints
- appropriate nullable rules

Do not replace relational design with JSON.

JSON is acceptable where structure is genuinely flexible.

==================================================
68. PUBLIC IDENTIFIERS
==================================================

Where enumeration risk or UX benefits exist, consider:

ULID
UUID

for public-facing resources.

Do not assume UUID/ULID provides authorization.

Policies remain mandatory.

==================================================
69. BUSINESS CONFIGURATION
==================================================

Business settings Beacon needs to modify should not require code
changes.

Examples:

- compliance requirements
- eligibility rules
- readiness rules
- guidance
- deadlines
- knowledge content

Make them configurable.

==================================================
70. VERSIONING
==================================================

Requirements used in historical assessments must be versioned.

If Beacon changes a compliance rule tomorrow, an assessment performed
yesterday must still reference yesterday's rule version.

Store effective versions.

==================================================
71. AI ARCHITECTURE
==================================================

Do not scatter AI calls across controllers.

Create a dedicated abstraction.

Example:

AIServiceInterface

or appropriate provider architecture.

Domain AI capabilities:

InvoiceExtractionService
TransactionCategorizationService
FinancialInsightService
KnowledgeAssistantService

Provider adapters may include:

OpenAIProvider

Future providers must be replaceable without rewriting business
modules.

==================================================
72. AI SAFETY
==================================================

AI MAY:

- extract invoice fields
- suggest transaction categories
- explain trends
- answer questions using approved Beacon knowledge
- suggest next actions

AI MUST NOT:

- automatically post financial transactions
- change financial balances
- approve financing
- approve compliance
- change permissions
- alter eligibility rules
- release institutional referrals
- execute irreversible actions

==================================================
73. HUMAN REVIEW
==================================================

AI suggestions must visibly say they require review.

Example:

اقتراح ذكي — يرجى مراجعته قبل الاعتماد

OCR results must appear in an editable preview.

User may:

- confirm
- correct
- reject

Do not silently save AI extraction as final financial data.

==================================================
74. AI DATA MINIMIZATION
==================================================

Only send data required for the AI task.

Example:

Invoice OCR should not send an entire SME profile unless technically
necessary.

Never send:

- passwords
- tokens
- application secrets
- unrelated business information
- unrelated personal information

==================================================
75. AI RATE LIMITING
==================================================

Apply rate limits to expensive endpoints.

Examples:

AI assistant
OCR
document analysis
large AI reports

Protect against:

- abuse
- accidental loops
- excessive API cost

==================================================
76. KNOWLEDGE BASE
==================================================

Beacon-approved knowledge must be configurable.

Support:

- draft
- published
- archived
- versioning
- effective dates where needed

AI should use approved/published knowledge only in Beacon knowledge
mode.

==================================================
77. AI FAILURE
==================================================

AI failure must not break the application.

If the AI provider is unavailable:

users can still:

- add transactions
- review records
- create reports
- manage profiles
- complete compliance workflows
- complete readiness workflows
- use non-AI referral functions

Display simple error messages.

Do not show provider stack traces.

==================================================
78. QUEUES
==================================================

Use Laravel queues for:

- OCR
- AI requests
- large exports
- long-running reports
- notifications
- document processing

Initially use:

database queue

unless requirements prove otherwise.

Jobs should:

- handle retries
- handle failures
- avoid duplicate side effects
- not expose secrets

==================================================
79. JOB IDEMPOTENCY
==================================================

Where possible, jobs should be idempotent.

Retrying an OCR or AI job must not create duplicate business records.

==================================================
80. SCHEDULER
==================================================

Use Laravel Scheduler for:

- reminders
- deadline notifications
- cleanup jobs
- scheduled checks
- periodic reporting

Use one normal cron entry where possible.

==================================================
81. CACHING
==================================================

Use Laravel's cache abstraction.

Do not require Redis initially.

Cache only stable or safely invalidated data.

Never cache one user's sensitive data globally.

==================================================
82. PERFORMANCE
==================================================

Prevent N+1 queries.

Use:

- eager loading
- pagination
- indexed columns
- scoped queries

Do not load 10,000 records into React.

Use server-side pagination.

Optimize images/documents where appropriate.

==================================================
83. SECURITY HEADERS
==================================================

Evaluate and configure appropriate production headers such as:

Content-Security-Policy

X-Content-Type-Options

Referrer-Policy

Permissions-Policy

frame-ancestors / clickjacking protection

Test headers before deployment.

==================================================
84. PRODUCTION ERRORS
==================================================

Production:

APP_DEBUG=false

Never expose:

- stack traces
- environment values
- paths
- SQL
- secrets
- third-party credentials

==================================================
85. LOGGING
==================================================

Never log:

- passwords
- MFA codes
- authorization headers
- API keys
- session tokens
- secret credentials

Avoid logging complete sensitive request bodies.

==================================================
86. AUDIT LOG
==================================================

Audit important business/security actions.

At minimum:

- financial corrections
- administrative changes
- requirement changes
- scoring changes
- consent actions
- referral creation
- referral sharing
- permission changes
- account activation/deactivation
- sensitive administrative actions

Audit record should identify:

WHO
WHAT
WHEN
TARGET

Where useful:

BEFORE
AFTER

Never store secrets in audit logs.

==================================================
87. CONSENT
==================================================

Institutional referral sharing requires appropriate consent.

Store enough information to identify:

- consent type
- version
- timestamp
- actor
- scope
- current status

Withdrawal must prevent future sharing according to approved policy.

==================================================
88. MFI DATA ACCESS
==================================================

MFI users must never receive access to the full SME directory.

They may only access referral packages explicitly shared with their
institution.

This must be enforced in Laravel policies and tests.

==================================================
89. BACKUPS
==================================================

Configure regular production backups.

Target:

daily backups unless otherwise approved.

Back up:

- database
- required private files
- critical configuration where applicable

Backups must be protected.

==================================================
90. RESTORE TEST
==================================================

A backup is not considered valid until restoration has been tested.

Before final acceptance:

perform a documented restore test in a separate environment.

==================================================
91. ENVIRONMENTS
==================================================

Maintain separate:

development
staging
production

Do not casually use production data in development.

Never commit:

.env
credentials
API keys
private certificates

==================================================
92. TESTING STRATEGY
==================================================

Prioritize Laravel Feature Tests for critical workflows.

Add unit tests where business calculations benefit from isolated
testing.

Critical tests include:

Authentication
Authorization
Financial calculations
Consent
Referrals
Compliance configuration
Readiness rules
Document protection
AI fallback behavior

==================================================
93. SECURITY TESTS
==================================================

Required tests:

SME A cannot access SME B.

SME A cannot download SME B documents.

Advisor A cannot access unassigned SME B.

MFI A cannot access referral belonging to MFI B.

Normal users cannot access admin pages.

Normal users cannot change roles in request payloads.

Unauthorized exports fail.

Direct URL manipulation fails.

Disabled users lose access.

==================================================
94. RELEASE BLOCKERS
==================================================

Any of these are release-blocking:

- unauthorized record access
- unauthorized document access
- incorrect financial totals
- data loss
- broken required core workflow
- broken permission isolation
- unsafe file exposure
- broken backup restoration

Do not mark a release ready while these exist.

==================================================
95. FINANCIAL TESTS
==================================================

Test:

- income
- expenses
- sales
- purchases
- receivables
- payables
- partial payments
- credit transactions
- corrections
- exports
- reports
- multi-currency behavior if enabled

==================================================
96. MOBILE TESTING
==================================================

Every major SME workflow must be manually checked at:

360px
375px
390px
430px
768px
1024px
desktop

Verify:

- bottom nav
- app header
- keyboards
- forms
- dialogs
- cards
- scrolling
- sticky actions
- RTL
- long Arabic labels

==================================================
97. DESKTOP TESTING
==================================================

Desktop must be checked for:

- sidebar
- topbar
- wide tables
- responsive grids
- reports
- admin screens
- filters
- charts
- long forms

Avoid pages that feel empty on desktop because they were designed only
for mobile.

==================================================
98. COMPONENT QUALITY
==================================================

Avoid duplicated page structures.

Create reusable layouts and components.

But do NOT over-generalize components until there is a real reuse case.

Readable duplication is sometimes better than an incomprehensible
generic component.

==================================================
99. CONTROLLER RULE
==================================================

Controllers must remain thin.

Controller responsibilities:

- receive request
- authorize
- validation through FormRequest
- call action/service
- return Inertia response/redirect

Controllers must not contain complex financial or domain logic.

==================================================
100. ACTIONS AND SERVICES
==================================================

Prefer explicit classes for important operations.

Examples:

CreateTransactionAction

CorrectTransactionAction

CreateSMEAction

CalculateIncomeStatement

CalculateReadinessScore

CreateReferralAction

WithdrawConsentAction

Names must describe actual business behavior.

==================================================
101. DATABASE TRANSACTIONS
==================================================

Use DB::transaction() when multiple database changes must be atomic.

Examples:

Posting financial transaction

Creating referral package and consent snapshot

Complex administrative configuration publication

==================================================
102. DESTRUCTIVE OPERATIONS
==================================================

Do not hard-delete important:

- financial records
- compliance history
- assessments
- audit records

unless approved retention policy explicitly requires it.

Prefer where appropriate:

archive
void
reverse
deactivate
soft delete

==================================================
103. CONFIRMATIONS
==================================================

Require explicit confirmation for dangerous actions.

Examples:

Delete document

Withdraw consent

Release referral

Deactivate user

Final financial posting if relevant

Do not annoy users with confirmation for ordinary harmless saves.

==================================================
104. USER FRIENDLY LANGUAGE
==================================================

Do not expose developer terminology to SME users.

Avoid labels like:

Entity ID
Transaction Object
Payload
Synchronization Queue
API Error

Use natural Arabic business language.

==================================================
105. SIMPLE UX RULE
==================================================

Every page should answer:

"What is the primary thing this user needs to do here?"

The main action should be recognizable within approximately 3 seconds.

==================================================
106. NON-TECHNICAL USER RULE
==================================================

Whenever there are two UX solutions, choose the one that requires less
technical knowledge.

Avoid expecting users to understand:

accounting system terminology
technical workflow concepts
database structure
AI terminology

Explain complex concepts in plain Arabic.

==================================================
107. FIRST USE
==================================================

Important first-time workflows should provide lightweight guidance.

Do not create long tutorial popups.

Prefer:

- short help text
- empty-state instructions
- progress indicators
- contextual tips

==================================================
108. FILTERS
==================================================

Mobile filters should appear in an offcanvas/bottom sheet.

Desktop filters may appear inline.

Do not occupy half the mobile screen permanently with filters.

==================================================
109. DESKTOP / MOBILE ROUTES
==================================================

Do not create separate routes like:

/mobile/transactions
/desktop/transactions

Use the same route:

/transactions

and adapt presentation responsively.

==================================================
110. PWA INSTALLATION
==================================================

Where PWA support is enabled:

Users may install the platform on their phone home screen.

The installed application should:

- launch in standalone mode where supported
- use application icons
- use Beacon theme color
- respect authenticated sessions securely

Do not create custom insecure session storage to support PWA.

==================================================
111. NO LOCALSTORAGE FOR SENSITIVE DATA
==================================================

Do not store:

- auth tokens
- passwords
- financial documents
- complete sensitive SME profiles

in localStorage.

For offline drafts, store only approved minimum fields using an
appropriate controlled storage approach.

Assume mobile devices may be shared.

==================================================
112. CLIENT STORAGE
==================================================

Any offline client-side storage must be:

- minimal
- documented
- scoped
- removable after synchronization where appropriate

Assume mobile devices may be shared.

==================================================
113. DESIGN RESPONSIVENESS
==================================================

Do NOT simply reduce font size on mobile.

Mobile must have its own intentional information architecture.

Desktop:

sidebar
topbar
tables
multi-column cards

Mobile:

app header
bottom navigation
single column
cards/lists
sticky actions
offcanvas filters

Same system.
Different presentation.

==================================================
114. COLOR USAGE
==================================================

Use semantic color consistently.

Success:
green family

Warning:
amber/yellow family

Danger:
red family

Primary:
Beacon brand family

Never use color alone to communicate meaning.

==================================================
115. ICONS
==================================================

Use Bootstrap Icons.

Avoid mixing multiple icon libraries.

Always pair unfamiliar icons with text.

==================================================
116. CHARTS
==================================================

If charts are required, use a free open-source library such as Chart.js.

Keep charts:

- simple
- readable
- responsive
- accessible

Do not use 3D charts.

Do not show charts when a number or simple table communicates better.

==================================================
117. EXPORTS
==================================================

Support where required:

PDF
Excel
CSV

Every export must apply the same authorization scope as the visible
data.

Never trust an SME ID passed from the frontend without authorization.

==================================================
118. DOCUMENT PREVIEW
==================================================

Document previews must not expose direct public URLs.

Authorization applies before preview as well as download.

==================================================
119. SEARCH
==================================================

Start with MySQL filtering/search.

Do not introduce:

Elasticsearch
Meilisearch
Algolia

without measured need.

==================================================
120. BUSINESS DECISION RULE
==================================================

Never invent unclear business rules.

If information is missing, write:

TODO: BUSINESS DECISION REQUIRED

Then explain:

- missing decision
- available options
- technical impact

Do not silently invent financial, compliance or eligibility rules.

==================================================
121. BEFORE IMPLEMENTING A MODULE
==================================================

Before coding a major module, first provide a concise plan covering:

1. user workflow
2. database tables
3. models
4. relationships
5. permissions
6. policies
7. validations
8. security considerations
9. React pages/components
10. mobile behavior
11. desktop behavior
12. tests

Then implement.

==================================================
122. DO NOT CHANGE SCOPE
==================================================

Do not remove difficult contractual features.

Do not silently add major features.

If a feature significantly changes architecture or schedule:

explain the issue before implementing the change.

==================================================
123. DEFINITION OF DONE
==================================================

A feature is NOT complete because the happy path works.

A feature is complete only when:

- backend logic works
- Laravel validation exists
- authorization exists
- security has been considered
- React UI works
- Arabic RTL works
- mobile experience works
- desktop experience works
- loading state exists
- error state exists
- empty state exists
- audit logging exists when required
- relevant tests pass
- no unauthorized dependency was added
- no paid package was added
- code remains readable

==================================================
124. FINAL DESIGN RULE
==================================================

On mobile:

The user should feel they opened a mobile application.

On desktop:

The user should feel they opened a professional business web
application.

The mobile interface must NOT look like a compressed desktop ERP.

The desktop interface must NOT look like an enlarged phone screen.

Both experiences must use the same:

- backend
- business rules
- permissions
- data
- routes
- React application

but intentionally adapt their visual layout and interaction patterns.

==================================================
125. FINAL ENGINEERING PRINCIPLE
==================================================

Keep complexity in the backend where necessary.

Hide complexity from the user.

The final product should have:

Simple UX.

Strict authorization.

Auditable data.

Accurate financial logic.

Secure documents.

Minimal infrastructure.

Free/open-source dependencies.

Modern responsive design.

Mobile-app-like experience on phones.

Professional web-app experience on desktops.

Maintainable Laravel architecture.

Replaceable AI providers.

Full Beacon handover capability.
